/-!
# A unit Jacobian certificate implies affinoid regularity

Source: Temkin, Proposition 3.3.2, proof, printed pp. 20--21, the converse analytic
Jacobian criterion specialized to the ground field
(blueprint: lem-per-affinoid-jacobian-certificate-regular).
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace RestrictedSeries

/-- A unit analytic Jacobian certificate implies that the presented affinoid algebra is
regular Noetherian. No regularity, reducedness, perfectness, or characteristic assumption
is imposed on the quotient. Source: Temkin, Proposition 3.3.2, proof, pp. 20--21,
converse analytic Jacobian criterion specialized to the ground field
(blueprint: lem-per-affinoid-jacobian-certificate-regular).

Proof (the node's seven steps).
1. Apply lem-trf-affinoid-polynomial-closed-point-comparison with polynomial ideal zero
   to obtain that the Tate ring is Noetherian. Images of finite generators of inverse
   image ideals show its quotient B is Noetherian (def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring).
   The zero ring has no primes, so is regular (def-weil-regular-scheme).
2. For a maximal ideal m of B, pull it back to n in the Tate ring. Since the certificate
   is the unit ideal, some product aD lies outside n, with a in (I_E : I). In the local
   ring R at n, a is a unit, hence IR = I_E R, generated by the selected equations.
   Use def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime; no base-change identity for colon ideals is needed.
3. R is Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian. The comparison of Step 1
   identifies its completion with the completion of the polynomial ring at the contracted
   maximal ideal. Use lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-field-regular-ring,
   lem-weil-pbc-completion-regular-local, and lem-trf-regular-local-ring-isomorphism
   to conclude that R is regular local, without assuming the residue field separable.
4. The coefficient formula and the ultrametric bound on integer norms show that partial
   derivatives preserve restricted series. Finite Cauchy sums give Leibniz. The quotient
   rule extends them to R; differentiation of the fraction equality verifies well-definedness.
   Reduction modulo its maximal ideal q gives derivations to the residue field, vanishing
   on q squared and inducing a residue-field-linear map q/q² to the coordinate vector space.
5. Apply that map to a relation between the classes of the selected equations and project
   onto U. The resulting matrix has nonzero determinant, since D is outside n.
   The determinant formula commutes with reduction (def-weil-pbc-matrix-determinant),
   and lem-weil-pbc-matrix-adjugate-identity forces every coefficient to vanish.
   Thus the selected classes are independent, including the empty family.
6. Successively quotient R by these equations. Independence modulo q² implies that each
   next equation is in the current maximal ideal but outside its square. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-hypersurface-criterion at every step. The final ring is R/IR,
   identified with B localized at m by lem-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime-of-quotient;
   transfer regularity using lem-trf-regular-local-ring-isomorphism.
7. For any prime l of B choose a maximal ideal containing it by
   lem-weil-pbc-proper-ideal-contained-in-maximal. Apply lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-localization
   inside B localized at m. The fractions identification supplied by
   lem-weil-pbc-localization-of-localization identifies this further localization with B at l.
   Use lem-trf-regular-local-ring-isomorphism and def-weil-regular-scheme. Empty variable
   and relation families require no extra step; the argument uses no characteristic condition.
-/
theorem affinoid_jacobian_certificate_regular
    (F : Type u) [NormedField F] [IsUltrametricDist F] [CompleteSpace F]
    (hF : ∃ a : F, a ≠ 0 ∧ ‖a‖ ≠ 1) (r s : ℕ)
    (f : Fin s → ↥(MvPowerSeries.IsRestricted.subring (R := F) (fun _ : Fin r ↦ (1 : ℝ))))
    (hH : jacobianIdeal f = ⊤) :
    IsRegularRing
      (↥(MvPowerSeries.IsRestricted.subring (R := F) (fun _ : Fin r ↦ (1 : ℝ))) ⧸
        Ideal.span (Set.range f)) := by
  sorry

end RestrictedSeries
